<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tycho Brahe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1546">1546</a>–<a href="/years/1601">1601</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tycho Brahe was a Danish nobleman and astronomer. His observation of a new star in 1572 challenged the belief that the heavens were unchangeable. From his observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> on Ven, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, he made the most precise naked-eye measurements of his time. In 1599 he became imperial mathematician to Rudolf II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where his data passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe.jpg" alt="Tycho Brahe" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4tt_av_Tycho_Brahe_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_90153.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tycho was born Tyge Ottesen Brahe on 14 December 1546 at his family's seat at Knutstorp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, then Danish, the oldest of twelve siblings. He was heir to several of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s most influential noble families, and both his grandfathers and all his great-grandfathers had served on the king's Privy Council. In 1566 he went to study at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock">University of Rostock</a>. On 29 December of that year he lost part of his nose in a sword duel with his third cousin Manderup Parsberg, after a drunken quarrel over who was the better mathematician, and he wore a prosthetic nose for the rest of his life. His family had expected him to enter politics or law but supported his choice of the sciences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Tycho devoted himself to more accurate instruments, enlarging the sextant and the quadrant, and mounted them underground on the bedrock once he realised that wind and the movement of buildings affected them. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> granted him an estate on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ven-island">Hven</a> and money to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a>, the first large observatory in Christian Europe. In De nova stella of 1573 he refuted the Aristotelian belief in an unchanging celestial realm, and he showed that comets were not atmospheric phenomena. He could not reconcile Copernican heliocentrism with Aristotelian physics, so he devised a system in which the Sun and Moon orbit the Earth and the other planets orbit the Sun.</p><p>After Frederick's death in 1588 Tycho's influence at court declined, and in 1597 he was forced by the new king to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. He was invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where he became imperial astronomer and built an observatory at Benátky nad Jizerou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tycho fell ill after a banquet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and died eleven days later, on 24 October 1601, aged 54. Before dying he urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> to finish the Rudolphine Tables using Tycho's own planetary system. Kepler used Tycho's records of the motion of Mars to deduce his laws of planetary motion. Tycho's planetary model was soon discredited, but his observations were an essential contribution to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a>. Danish scholarship often depicted him as a mediocre scholar and a traitor to the nation, but from the second half of the 20th century scholars reassessed his significance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tycho Brahe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludvig Holberg, born in Bergen, wrote comedies and histories that founded modern Danish-Norwegian literature, and the astronomer <b>Tycho Brahe</b>, supported by Frederick II at his observatory of Uraniborg on Hven, made the most precise naked-eye observations of his age; Ole Rømer first measured the speed of light in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Rudolf II, Prague drew artists, alchemists and the astronomers <b>Tycho Brahe</b> and Johannes Kepler, and the Defenestration of Prague of 1618 set off the Thirty Years War, which reshaped Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tycho Brahe</b>’s observatory of Uraniborg on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II in 1576, produced the observations from which Johannes Kepler derived his laws of planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1572 <b>Tycho Brahe</b> observed a brilliant new star and showed that it lay beyond the Moon, and in 1577 that a comet did too, so that the heavens were neither unchanging nor made of solid spheres.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Work began on <b>Tycho Brahe</b>'s observatory on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II of Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Knutstorp Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.98,13.14&amp;year=1581" aria-label="Knutstorp Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4836 257 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tycho Brahe"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5286 527h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5286 527h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.98,13.14&amp;year=1581">What was here in 1581? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
